Car sales in China shine as rest of world reels from virus
Demand for cars in China is going from strength to strength, making the automobile market in Asia’s biggest economy a lone bright spot as the coronavirus pandemic puts a damper on sales in Europe and the U.S.
Passenger vehicle deliveries to dealers rose 8 percent to 2.1 million units, while total vehicle sales, including trucks and buses, expanded 13 percent to 2.57 million, data released later by the China Association of Automobile Manufacturers showed.
Automakers worldwide have invested billions of dollars in China, the world’s top car market since 2009, where the middle class is expanding but penetration is still relatively low. Brands from countries such as Germany and Japan have weathered the pandemic better than their local rivals — the combined market share of Chinese brands fell to 36.2 percent in the first eight months from a peak of 43.9 percent in 2017.
Regardless, China’s importance is boosted by its focus on nurturing the electric-car ecosystem, a technology shift in which automakers have invested a great deal of time and money. Beijing wants new-energy vehicles to account for 15 percent or more of the market in 2025, and at least half of all sales a decade later.
